{{$NEXT}}
  - Introduce the SimpiCI distribution with normalized events, exact detached
    checkouts, filesystem state and public JSON/log reports. Local logs are
    unredacted; worker uploads redact assigned secret values.
  - Poll GitHub and Forgejo mirrors with independent observations, including
    newly appearing refs after initial polling. Shared durable run
    deduplication is provided by the dispatcher queue. A repository whose refs
    cannot be read (git ls-remote fails), or that returns none of the
    configured refs although its last poll saw some, does not stop simpicid: it
    logs one "not polled" line naming the repository, its clone URL and the
    reason, polls the remaining repositories, keeps
    the recorded tips and tries again in the next cycle, so refs that return
    are not built as new. A repository that has no refs at all at its first
    poll, such as a mirror before its first synchronisation, is not polled
    either ("repository has no refs yet"): it gets no baseline and is read
    again in the next cycle, so its refs are a first observation when they
    arrive and build_initial decides. A project created empty with
    build_initial: false does not build its first push. A repository that has
    refs, but none of the configured ones, gets an empty baseline. A mirror
    that is only partly synchronised is not recognised and must be complete
    before it is polled. simpicid --once polls every repository and exits
    with 1 if one was not polled. SimpiCI::Source::GitPoll offers observe and
    rejection; poll accepts an observation. The poller merges an observation
    into the recorded tips instead of replacing them: a ref that disappears
    while others stay keeps its last tip, so back on the same commit it
    starts no run, on another commit it does. The state of a repository never
    drops a ref by itself; simpici --forget takes one out.
  - The poller locks the recorded tips of a repository from reading to
    writing, so a second simpicid on the same root loses no tip and builds
    nothing twice, and writes them only when they change. The state also
    lists the refs the last poll did not see. simpici --config FILE --state
    shows what is recorded for each repository; simpici --config FILE
    --repository NAME --forget REF takes one ref out (exit 0 forgotten, 1 not
    recorded, 2 repository not configured). A forgotten ref the repository
    still has is built as new at the next poll. The "not polled" line for a
    repository that returns no refs reads "configured refs seen at the last
    poll: N", counts only such refs and is a signal, no protection. A ref
    outside the refs filter keeps its tip. The dispatcher image has a
    simpici wrapper.
  - simpicid gives up on a repository whose refs are not read within
    ls_remote_timeout seconds (new top-level setting, default 60, shared by
    all queries made for one repository in a cycle), logs it as
    not polled and goes on with the next one. git ls-remote runs in its own
    process group that is ended together with its helpers, and it does not
    answer prompts on the terminal. A remote that prints a lot on standard
    error does not block the poller.
  - simpicid checks every configured repository when it starts, in local and
    in dispatcher mode, and exits before it polls if one is refused. The
    repositories setting must be a list of objects, each with a nonempty name
    and clone_url; an entry of another shape, such as a bare URL, is refused
    by its position, and a configuration without repositories does not start,
    in local mode either. A clone URL is refused when it is http:// or
    https:// with any user part, when it carries a password in the user part
    in any other form (ssh://user:password@..., ftp(s)://, a URL behind a
    remote helper, an empty password, a percent-encoded colon,
    user:password@host:path), or when it contains whitespace or control
    characters. A user name alone stays valid (ssh://git@host/path,
    git@host:path). The message names the repository and its position in the
    configuration and never repeats the value; credentials belong in a Git
    credential helper of the account that runs git. The rule is
    SimpiCI::Event->clone_url_rejection; clone_url_without_credentials gives
    the URL as shown in the "not polled" line. The runner passes the clone
    URL to git remote add after "--", so a URL that begins with "-" is not
    read as an option.
  - Dispatch durable leased jobs to outbound SSH workers, with fenced recovery
    and idempotent completion upload through a restricted worker protocol.
  - Scope dispatcher secret files to repositories, refs, events and publish or
    deploy phases; redact uploaded logs and keep values out of command lines.
    A grant ref is exact or a refs/<path>/* pattern (refs/tags/* covers every
    release tag); any other use of "*" is rejected as a configuration error.
    Every grant is checked when simpicid starts in dispatcher mode and before
    each claim: an unusable one (name, ref pattern, phases, secret file) is
    reported with its repository and secret, simpicid does not start, and no
    lease is taken, while completions are still accepted. A secret name may
    not be any variable the executor assigns itself (CICD_BRANCH, CICD_TAG,
    CICD_REGISTRY, CICD_REGISTRY_USER, ...); only CICD_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
    passes through. In dispatcher mode simpicid needs read access to the
    granted secret files.
  - simpici-worker reports a claim it cannot execute as a failed run with
    exit code 125 instead of leaving it to its lease: an event it refuses, a
    claim of the wrong shape, secret files it cannot write, a failure of the
    run supervisor. The uploaded log ends with "SimpiCI::Worker run N
    aborted: <reason>", where the reason is one of nine fixed ones
    (SimpiCI::Worker->abort_reasons): no path below the worker root, no file
    or line of the installation and no value of the claim is published. The
    error itself is written to standard error of the worker, in the same
    line, with assigned secret values redacted. The event and the timeout
    are checked before any secret file is written, so a claim without an
    integer timeout is refused first, and secrets/<run>/ is removed whichever
    way the run ends. A claim whose run is not a run number is refused before
    anything is written.
  - The runner makes the checkout quietly, so that a log no longer begins
    with the path of the state root, and a command that cannot be started is
    logged as "SimpiCI::Runner cannot start git" or "cannot start the
    executor" instead of a Perl warning with the path of the installation.
    One event is one line on standard error of simpici-worker.
  - Worker and dispatcher redact with one function (SimpiCI::Role::Secrets):
    every occurrence of every assigned value, also where one value begins
    another or two overlap; empty values do not break it. A [REDACTED] marker
    in a log is never searched for secret values, so the dispatcher's second
    pass leaves the worker's markers alone, and the worker does not cut a log
    in the middle of one; a secret value that contains [REDACTED] is
    rejected. The dispatcher removes claims/<run>.json once a completion is
    recorded; a completion without a snapshot is recorded with its log
    withheld. The worker removes
    orphaned secrets/* before it contacts the dispatcher, and a claim whose
    secret is not one NAME=VALUE line fails the run with exit 125.
    SimpiCI::Queue gains leased.
  - simpicid in dispatcher mode ends leases that ran out at the start of every
    polling cycle, also with --once: the run becomes interrupted and its
    secret snapshot is removed without a worker having to ask, and every
    worker request does the same first. A claim that never reached its worker
    ends that way too; deploy/README.md describes how to build such a commit
    again.
  - simpici-dispatch limits only the reading of a request (30 s, top-level
    setting request_read_timeout); a request that was read is served to its
    end, however long redaction takes.
  - A completion the dispatcher can never accept (unknown run, stale claim,
    expired claim) is answered with {"rejected": reason}. The worker moves it
    to rejected/<run>.json, names it in one line on standard error and claims
    work again; a failed connection or dispatcher keeps completion.json and
    is retried. An ssh that ends before it read the request no longer kills
    the worker with SIGPIPE. SimpiCI::Queue gains refusal and
    refusal_reasons.
  - A checkout command or executor that a signal ended is reported as
    signalled with exit code 128 plus the signal; the checkout stops there
    and the executor does not start.
  - The worker keeps no log and no files of a run: public/runs/<run>.log,
    work/<run>, tmp/<run> and runs/<run> are removed once the completion with
    the redacted log is saved, and what a killed worker or an earlier version
    left is removed at the start of every cycle. The worker refuses the state
    root of a dispatcher or daemon.
  - simpici-worker, a local simpicid and simpici end a run on TERM, INT or
    HUP, with the process group of the executor and its containers, and
    report it as signalled; a killed supervisor leaves a process that does
    the same. Job containers carry the labels simpici.instance and
    simpici.run, only containers with the label of the own state root are
    removed, and the worker removes orphaned ones at its start.
    TimeoutStopSec of the worker unit is 60.
  - Discover repository jobs from their filenames, execute them in selected
    containers and enforce ordered phase barriers with bounded same-phase
    concurrency (default 2).
  - Expose the artifacts of earlier phases to later jobs: every job gets
    CICD_INPUTS=/inputs with <phase>/<job>/ of each earlier phase mounted
    read-only. Jobs of the same phase never see each other, a job that wrote
    nothing leaves an empty directory, and nothing is transferred between
    workers or to the dispatcher.
  - Merge repository jobs and provider-generated jobs in a writable staging
    directory: operator-selected provider images (SIMPICI_PROVIDERS, exposed as
    the reusable workflow's "provider" input) generate additional .cicd/*.sh
    files, and the repository's own same-named jobs always win. Providers run
    read-only over the checkout with no registry credentials or Docker socket,
    letting a repository ship no .cicd at all.
  - Provide a reusable GitHub/Forgejo action and GitHub workflow for the same
    zero-configuration repository-job contract used by the native daemon.
  - Add complete POD manuals and conventional help output for both command-line
    programs, including explicit executor-path configuration.
  - Put reusable core classes under SimpiCI::* and command implementations
    under SimpiCI::App::*.
  - Install the shared phase executor as simpici-executor so CPAN, containers,
    GitHub and Forgejo use the same runnable implementation.
  - Expand the README, cookbook and operations reference with executable
    examples, distinguish current behavior from the planned branch policy,
    and remove obsolete planning notes.
  - Consolidate project agent instructions and skills under CLAUDE.md and
    .claude with no duplicate agent configuration.
  - Publish the daemon image primarily as raudssus/simpici on Docker Hub and
    mirror it to GHCR with independent CI jobs.
  - Run the dispatcher as a container (deploy/dispatcher): an image without
    Docker CLI, a Compose file with the poller and a restricted sshd endpoint
    (port 2222) for worker forced commands, plus example dispatcher
    configuration and authorized_keys. A .dockerignore keeps its state,
    secrets, SSH material and configuration out of every build context.
    Documented as section 5 of deploy/README.md.
